The incoming iOS 27.2 and iPadOS 27.2 updates enable Apple's Hearing Aid and Hearing Test features on the AirPods Pro 2 and AirPods Pro 3 <a href="https://www.apple.com/ca/airpods-pro/feature-availability/">in five Canadian provinces</a>: Alberta, British Columbia, New Brunswick, Prince Edward Island, and Saskatchewan. The features are still not available in any other provinces, but Ontario is <a href="https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/toronto/ont-hearing-aids-9.7389328">reportedly about to change a law</a> that will pave the way for the features to launch there.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2024/09/airpods-pro-2-hearing-aids.jpg" alt="" width="1600" height="900" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-964676" /><br/>
"The Hearing Test and Hearing Aid features are supported in select provinces in Canada on AirPods Pro 2 and later with the latest firmware when paired with a compatible iPhone or iPad with iOS 27.2 or iPadOS 27.2 and later," says Apple.<br/>
<br/>
iOS 27.2 and iPadOS 27.2 are currently in beta and could be released as soon as today.<br/>
<br/>
With the clinical-grade Hearing Aid feature, AirPods Pro 2 and later can amplify the sound around you. Apple says the feature, available at no additional cost, is intended for adults with perceived mild to moderate hearing loss.<br/>
<br/>
Meanwhile, the five-minute Hearing Test allows adults to identify if they have hearing loss by measuring their ability to hear different frequencies of sound. Afterwards, the results will show a hearing loss classification and recommended next steps.<br/>
<br/>
The two features first launched in the U.S. and select other countries <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2024/10/21/airpods-pro-hearing-features-launching-in-ios-18-1/">in October 2024</a>.<br/>
<br/>
